FEATURE: modal-example-expansion
DATE: 2026-08-04
SCOPE: multiple modals in the example, expose the blur overlay option, update the guide
FOLLOWS: changelog/modal-scroll-lock

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected.

No module behaviour changed. vUX-modalDisplayer.js is untouched: the example now
exercises capabilities the module already had but that nothing demonstrated. The one
library file modified is assets/css/modalDisplayer.css, for a one line overlay
positioning fix contributed during this round.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The example went from three modals to seven, each chosen to show something the
others do not:

  loginModal    page trigger; nested triggers for reset and self-open
  nestedModal   opened from loginModal
  termsModal    opened from nestedModal, making the chain three layers deep
  profileModal  a second, independent page entry point
  confirmModal  a confirm stacked over a form
  helpModal     opened by a SECOND ModalDisplayer instance, onto the same stack
  tallModal     taller than the viewport

Added alongside them:

- An overlay background selector exposing config.overlayBackgroundType. Blur has
  existed since v4 and is listed in ChangeLog.md, but nothing in the repository
  showed it working.
- A second ModalDisplayer instance with its own trigger class, effect and tint. This
  is the only way to observe, in a browser, that the stack is shared across instances
  and that one Escape pops exactly one layer — behaviour the guide asserts and
  nothing demonstrated.
- A live "Open layers" counter bound to `depth`, so stacking is displayed rather
  than inferred.
- Distinct accent colours per modal, so stacked layers are distinguishable at a
  glance.

Existing ids, classes and structure were preserved deliberately, since the automated
suites drive this page as their fixture.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING: closeAll() was demonstrated in a place it could never work
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The first version of this change put a closeAll() button on the page. The automated
run failed on it: Playwright timed out clicking it, reporting that the overlay
intercepted pointer events.

That is correct behaviour, not a defect — a modal blocks the page beneath it — but it
made the control useless exactly when it had a purpose, since closeAll() only matters
while a stack is open. The button moved inside the two deepest modals, where the top
layer makes it reachable, and the page text now explains why. A dead control became a
demonstration of modality, and the suite asserts both halves: that the page behind is
not hit-testable, and that closeAll() from inside the top layer unwinds the stack.

The listener is delegated from document rather than bound to the button, because the
component copies modal content into the overlay and a listener on the original node
would not exist on the copy the user clicks. The example now follows the advice its
own guide gives consumers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GUIDE UPDATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The blur section gained three facts that are not deducible from the API, since every
config property is write-only:

- config.overlayStyle is silently ignored in blur mode, because the module only
  applies an overlay background when the type is "color".
- The blur is reference-checked against the whole stack, so a nested open and close
  does not flicker it off and on.
- Config is snapshotted per layer, so switching the mode applies from the next open
  rather than to the modal already displayed. Without this the example's control
  reads as broken.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CSS FIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.vModal is position:fixed with top:0 and width:100% but had no horizontal anchor. A
fixed element with `left: auto` sits at its static position rather than the viewport
edge, which is invisible on a plain body but breaks as soon as the body has a left
margin or padding. `left:0` was added. It matters more since stacking, because every
open modal now creates its own overlay and would reproduce any offset on every layer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Example demo suite (new)   31 passed, 0 failed
  Main suite                 71 passed, 0 failed
  Stacking edge suite        16 passed, 0 failed
  Scroll-lock suite           9 passed, 0 failed

The new suite drives the demo the way a user would: opening each modal from the page,
building the three-deep chain by clicking through it, checking the depth readout,
confirming the page behind is not clickable, running closeAll() from inside the top
layer, stacking across the two instances, and exercising both overlay modes —
including that the modal itself is not blurred, that the overlay is left untinted in
blur mode, and that the blur is held across a nested open and lifted only when the
stack empties.

Testing note: an Escape pressed while a close animation is still running is correctly
ignored rather than queued. The first version of the new suite used fixed delays and
tripped over this against the alt instance's `flip` effect (~600ms), leaving a layer
open and timing out later. The suite now waits on stack depth rather than on elapsed
time, which is both correct and faster.
